  "body": "Coming from [this Firefox bug report](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1475332#c8), where they assert that Chrome is doing it wrong, given the following code:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nclass MyDiv extends HTMLDivElement {\r\n  // constructor() { console.log(super().localName); }\r\n}\r\ncustomElements.define('my-div', MyDiv, {extends: 'div'});\r\n\r\nconsole.log((document.body.appendChild(\r\n  document.createElement('div', {is: 'my-div'})\r\n)).outerHTML);\r\n\r\nconsole.log((document.body.appendChild(\r\n  new MyDiv\r\n)).outerHTML);\r\n```\r\n\r\nChrome would produce, after logging `div` if commented out:\r\n```\r\n<div is=\"my-div\"></div>\r\n<div is=\"my-div\"></div>\r\n```\r\n\r\nwhile Firefox + Nightly would produce, after logging `div` if commented out:\r\n```\r\n<div is=\"my-div\"></div>\r\n<div></div>\r\n```\r\n\r\nBy specifications, asking users to remember to set attribute on every builtin constructor makes no sense for the following reasons:\r\n\r\n  * the constructor is invoked for element already present on the DOM (setting attribute twice makes no sense)\r\n  * it's easily a footgun for any styling related issue such `[is=\"my-component\"]`\r\n\r\nPlease amend current specifications to automatically set the attribute when an element is created procedurally, thank you.",
